# G097 — Image Map Generator

*v2. See `image-map.md` for prose: shape polymorphism as one `contains`
predicate, ray-casting point-in-polygon with even-odd rule, z-order
resolves overlaps via stable sort, HTML `<map>` as universal export.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Dashboard Hot Spots

[vault-dashboard @layout
    IN ORDER TO
        build an image map with rect/circle/polygon regions
        dispatch user clicks to the topmost region that claims the point
        and persist the same map as an HTML <map> document

    @map <- [im[new]]

    [im[add {map: @map,
              region: {id: 1,
                       shape: [im[rect   {x: 0,   y: 0,   w: 200, h: 100}]],
                       action: "/tasks",   alt: "Tasks", z: 0}}]]
    [im[add {map: @map,
              region: {id: 2,
                       shape: [im[rect   {x: 200, y: 0,   w: 200, h: 100}]],
                       action: "/projects", alt: "Projects", z: 0}}]]
    [im[add {map: @map,
              region: {id: 3,
                       shape: [im[circle {cx: 150, cy: 150, r: 30}]],
                       action: "/urgent",   alt: "Urgent", z: 10}}]]

    sync @user-events {
        click @x @y ->
            @region <- [im[hit-test {map: @map, x: @x, y: @y}]]
            @region
                where exists -> [navigate {to: @region:action}]
                otherwise    -> [ui[toast {msg: "no region"}]]
    }

    [vault[save {path: "ui/dashboard.html",
                  content: [im[to-html {map: @map, name: "dashboard"}]]}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[im
    rect           {x, y, w, h}
    circle         {cx, cy, r}
    polygon        {points}
    contains       {shape, x, y}
    bbox           {shape}
    new
    add            {map, region}
    hit-test       {map, x, y}                    ;; -> Region | none
    hit-test-all   {map, x, y}                    ;; -> [Region]
    to-html        {map, name}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: hit test as `where contains` with z-order

[im[hit-test {map: @m, x: @x, y: @y}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return the topmost region that claims the point
        so overlapping regions resolve by the usual UI stacking rule

    @m:regions
        where [im[contains {shape: @region:shape, x: @x, y: @y}]]
        ordered by z descending, insertion-order ascending (stable)
        first
        || -> none
]

Shape polymorphism is the contract: rect, circle, and polygon each
answer the same `contains` predicate through their own internal test.
The hit-test pipeline is uniform regardless of shape.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: polygon `contains` as ray-casting `where` odd-crossings

[im[contains {shape: @poly, x: @x, y: @y}]
    IN ORDER TO
        decide inside vs outside for a (possibly concave) polygon
        using the 1962 ray-casting algorithm every GIS/CAD tool uses

    shoot a horizontal ray from (@x, @y) going right
    count edges where (yi > @y) is not equal to (yj > @y)
        and the x-intersection falls to the right of @x
    where crossings count is odd    -> inside
    where crossings count is even   -> outside
]

Ray casting with the even-odd rule is the canonical point-in-polygon
algorithm. First published 1962; no improvement for general polygons.
Cross-language byte-identity is the Rosetta Stone verification.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @m <- [im[new]]
    [im[add {map: @m,
              region: {id: 1, shape: [im[rect {0, 0, 100, 100}]],
                        action: "/home", alt: "Home", z: 0}}]]
    [im[add {map: @m,
              region: {id: 2, shape: [im[circle {50, 50, 20}]],
                        action: "/center", alt: "Centre", z: 1}}]]

    -> [im[hit-test {map: @m, x: 50, y: 50}]]
    ;; -> {id: 2, action: "/center"}  — circle wins on z

    -> [im[hit-test {map: @m, x: 90, y: 90}]]
    ;; -> {id: 1, action: "/home"}   — only rect contains (90,90)
]
